-- The Network and Information Systems Regulations 2018 (S.I. 2018/506).
-- Incident notification: the NOUNS, and nothing else.
--
-- DRAFT. Not for use as advice. No domain expert and no lawyer qualified in the law of
-- any part of the United Kingdom has read this against the source.
-- The source is an AID (the Lex API's re-serving of legislation.gov.uk data); the
-- official text on legislation.gov.uk could not be read, and this was NOT checked
-- against it. See NOTES.md.
--
-- These are cyber-security regulations, NOT an AI law. Seat: a company that provides an
-- AI model service, asking whether these Regulations bind it and, if they do, what it
-- must notify, to whom, by when, and what can be done to it if it does not.
-- Every fact here is one that provider could state.
-- Source: ../../registers/source-bundle/uksi-2018-506.txt (src:N below is line N).

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `Nouns for the NIS Regulations 2018`

-- A provider's own answer to a classification the Regulations leave to the facts.
-- "I cannot say" is a real answer: regulation 1 says nothing about AI services, and
-- whether a hosted AI model or API is a "cloud computing service" or an "online search
-- engine" is left open by the text (fork F1). No default is supplied.
DECLARE `my answer to a classification` IS ONE OF
    `yes, it is`
    `no, it is not`
    `I cannot say`

-- Who I am, as regulation 1 and regulation 8 see me.
DECLARE `the provider as it describes itself` HAS
    -- regulation 1(2), "digital service": a service within point (b) of Article 1(1) of
    -- Directive 2015/1535 (an information society service), a borrowed term asked as a fact.
    `my service is an information society service within Article 1(1)(b) of Directive 2015/1535` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is my service a cloud computing service`                       IS A `my answer to a classification`
    `is my service an online search engine`                         IS A `my answer to a classification`
    `is my service an online marketplace`                           IS A `my answer to a classification`
    `I provide the service in the United Kingdom`                   IS A BOOLEAN
    `my head office is in the United Kingdom`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `I have nominated a representative established in the United Kingdom` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- regulation 1(3)(e)(ii): a borrowed definition (Commission Recommendation 2003/361/EC),
    -- not deposited here, so asked as a fact.
    `I am a micro or small enterprise as defined in Commission Recommendation 2003/361/EC` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- regulation 1(2) "OES" and regulation 8: whether I am designated, or deemed designated,
    -- as an operator of an essential service. Schedule 2's thresholds are deferred, so
    -- this is asked as a fact.
    `I am an OES, designated or deemed designated under regulation 8` IS A BOOLEAN

-- One incident, as I can describe it.
DECLARE `the event as I know it` HAS
    -- regulation 1(2), "incident": "any event having an actual adverse effect on the
    -- security of network and information systems".
    `the event had an actual adverse effect on the security of network and information systems` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- regulation 12(3) and (7): my own assessment, having taken into account the
    -- parameters regulation 12(7) lists.
    `the incident had a substantial impact on the provision of a digital service I provide` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- regulation 12(4)
    `I have access to information that enables me to assess whether the impact is substantial` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- regulation 11(1) and (2), for an OES.
    `the incident had a significant impact on the continuity of my essential service` IS A BOOLEAN
    -- regulation 12(9), for an OES that relies on an RDSP.
    `an incident affecting an RDSP I rely on had a significant impact on the continuity of my essential service` IS A BOOLEAN

-- A moment, on one clock. The Regulations name no time zone (fork F4); both moments
-- compared must be read on the same clock, e.g. both in UTC.
DECLARE `a moment` HAS
    `day`    IS A DATE
    `hour`   IS A NUMBER
    `minute` IS A NUMBER

-- The two notification duties with a 72-hour clock, and the one without.
DECLARE `a notification duty` IS ONE OF
    `regulation 11(1): an OES's NIS incident`
    `regulation 12(9): an OES's notice of an incident affecting an RDSP it relies on`
    `regulation 12(3): an RDSP's incident`

-- What went wrong with a notification, as the provider would describe it.
DECLARE `what happened to my notification` IS ONE OF
    `I notified in time with the required information`
    `I did not notify at all`
    `I notified, but more than 72 hours after the clock started`
    `I notified in time, but without the required information`
    -- regulation 12(6)(b), RDSPs only: not a 12(5) item, and not named in 17(2) (F3).
    `I notified in time with the regulation 12(5) items, but not enough for the Information Commission to determine the significance of any cross-border impact (regulation 12(6)(b))`

-- Whether an enforcement notice was served for the failure (regulation 17) and what it required.
DECLARE `the enforcement notice position` IS ONE OF
    `no enforcement notice was served on me`
    `an enforcement notice was served on me but required no steps`
    `an enforcement notice required steps and I took them within its period`
    `an enforcement notice required steps and I did not take them within its period`

-- The authority's determination under regulation 18(6)(c) and (d): a judgment the
-- Regulations give to the NIS enforcement authority, asked as a fact.
DECLARE `the authority's view of the contravention` IS ONE OF
    `the authority determines it has or could have created a significant risk to, or significant impact on, my service provision`
    `the authority determines it does not meet that test`
    `the authority has not yet determined this`

-- Fork F3: is a notification made after the 72 hours a failure to "notify an incident
-- under regulation 12(3)"? Regulation 17(2) does not list regulation 12(6). The same
-- two readings answer a 12(6)(b) shortfall (not enough on cross-border impact): on
-- reading A all of 12(6) qualifies the 12(3) duty; on reading B none of it is listed.
DECLARE `a reading of late RDSP notice` IS ONE OF
    `reading A: a late notice is a failure to notify under regulation 12(3)`
    `reading B: lateness alone is not a failure regulation 17(2) lists`
